//! crypto/hybrid_signed_sealed.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the HYBRID sealed-AND-signed door: a message sealed TO a hybrid identity AND signed BY a
//! hybrid identity, so one frame carries both CONFIDENTIALITY and PROVENANCE hardened on BOTH families at once — the classical Ed25519/X25519 world
//! Comlink uses today, AND the post-quantum ML-DSA/ML-KEM world a lattice identity carries. It completes the hybrid surface: crypto/hybrid_identity.rye
//! gives a keeper a name that signs on both families, crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye gives a message that hides on both, and this door gives one frame that does
//! both — the hybrid analog of crypto/kumara_pq_sealed.rye (which seals-and-signs on the post-quantum family alone). A forger who breaks one signature
//! family is caught; an eavesdropper who breaks one agreement family reads nothing; only an adversary who breaks BOTH families on BOTH sides defeats it.
//!
//! It authors NO new cryptography and adds NO new primitive. Confidentiality is crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye's own onion (the classical X25519 box inside the
//! post-quantum ML-KEM box — a message readable only when both agreements open); provenance is crypto/hybrid_identity.rye's own hybrid signature (an
//! Ed25519 signature beside an ML-DSA signature, genuine only when both verify). This module contributes only the framing that binds them — a signed
//! envelope around the hybrid sealed frame, opened verify-before-trust: the sender's two public keys are checked against the reader's expectation, and the
//! hybrid signature verified over a BLAKE2b-512 digest of the sealed frame, BEFORE either agreement is ever opened. A valid signature from the wrong
//! keeper is caught, never mistaken for the right one, exactly as crypto/kumara_pq_sealed.rye's binding catches it on the post-quantum family.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, audit-ready). Double-seats BESIDE crypto/kumara_pq_sealed.rye (the
//! post-quantum-only signed-and-sealed door) and crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye (the hybrid anonymous confidential door), retiring neither.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-195850_the-hybrid-sealed-door-both-agreements.md · active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-192400_post-quantum-identity-is-dual-key.md.
//! Kin: crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye (the confidentiality onion, GREEN) · crypto/hybrid_identity.rye (the hybrid signature, GREEN) · crypto/kumara_pq_sealed.rye
//! (the post-quantum-only sibling this mirrors) · crypto/blake2b.rye (the digest the signature covers). Clean-room: BIP-39, SLIP-0010, RFC 5869, FIPS
//! 203/204 are public standards studied through their specs; the framing is our own Rye wiring over our own GREEN rungs (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE ENVELOPE — magic "GHS1" ‖ sender-Ed25519-public(32) ‖ sender-ML-DSA-public(pk_len) ‖ Ed25519-signature(64) ‖ ML-DSA-signature(sig_len) ‖ inner
//!   hybrid_sealed frame(variable, to the end). The fixed-width header (magic, the sender's two public keys, the two signatures) sits FIRST, so open
//!   locates the trailing inner frame without parsing a nested length; the inner frame runs to the end and crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye validates it whole.
//!   The hybrid signature covers a BLAKE2b-512 digest of the inner sealed frame — a fixed 64 bytes, within ML-DSA's message bound at every plaintext
//!   length, bound to the exact ciphertext by the hash's collision resistance — so a tampered frame breaks the signature (caught at verify) or an AEAD tag
//!   (caught at open), and never opens.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. Every identity in the selftest is a TEST hybrid identity from a
//! published-vector mnemonic; the module's own functions call no std.crypto (each half is proven one rung down). Both the inner X25519 ephemeral and the
//! ML-KEM message must be fresh randomness in real use — the selftest supplies deterministic TEST values and names the duty. Constant-time timing-safety
//! stays crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md's named horizon; sealing to or signing as the maintainer's OWN hybrid identity stays the custody gate.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/hybrid_signed_sealed.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const hybrid = @import("hybrid_identity.rye");
const hsealed = @import("hybrid_sealed.rye");
const pq = @import("kumara_pq_identity.rye");
const classical = @import("kumara_identity.rye");
const blake2b = @import("blake2b.rye");

pub const hybrid_signed_sealed_version = "20260816.195850";

/// The four magic bytes opening every Grain hybrid sealed-and-signed envelope (v1).
pub const magic: [4]u8 = .{ 'G', 'H', 'S', '1' };
pub const magic_len: u32 = 4;

/// The Ed25519 public-key and signature widths the classical half carries — 32 and 64 bytes.
pub const ed_public_len: u32 = classical.public_len;
pub const ed_sig_len: u32 = hybrid.ed_sig_len;

/// The 32-byte inner ephemeral and KEM-message widths, borrowed from the sealed door so a caller sizes one value each.
pub const ephemeral_bytes: u32 = hsealed.ephemeral_bytes;
pub const seed_bytes: u32 = hsealed.seed_bytes;

/// header_len — the fixed-width envelope prefix for a parameter set: magic ‖ sender Ed25519 public ‖ sender ML-DSA public ‖ Ed25519 signature ‖ ML-DSA
/// signature. Everything after it is the inner hybrid_sealed frame.
pub fn header_len(comptime p: pq.PqParams) u32 {
    return magic_len + ed_public_len + pq.sign_public_len(p) + ed_sig_len + pq.sig_len(p);
}

/// The largest plaintext one signed-and-sealed carry holds — inherited from the inner hybrid sealed frame, so this door adds no looser limit.
pub const max_plain_bytes: u32 = hsealed.max_plain_bytes;

/// max_frame_bytes — the whole envelope at the largest plaintext, per set: the fixed header plus the inner hybrid sealed frame's own maximum. Named so a
/// caller's scratch cites one ceiling.
pub fn max_frame_bytes(comptime p: pq.PqParams) u32 {
    return header_len(p) + hsealed.max_frame_bytes(p);
}

pub const HybridSignedSealedError = error{
    /// seal's output buffer is too small to hold the whole envelope — refused before any byte the caller relies on.
    OutTooSmall,
    /// A plaintext longer than max_plain_bytes — refused before any key agreement or signing.
    PayloadTooLong,
    /// open was handed a buffer shorter than the fixed header, so it cannot hold even an empty inner frame.
    FrameTooShort,
    /// The first four bytes are not the GHS1 magic — this is not a hybrid sealed-and-signed envelope.
    BadMagic,
    /// The envelope names a sender that is not the one the reader expected — a valid signature from the wrong keeper, refused before any agreement is opened.
    UnexpectedSigner,
    /// The sender's hybrid signature did not verify over the inner sealed frame — one or both families failed, so the provenance is not genuine. No
    /// plaintext is revealed.
    BadSignature,
    /// The inner hybrid sealed frame did not open (a wrong recipient on one or both agreements, or a tampered ciphertext). No plaintext is revealed.
    OpenFailed,
    /// A composed seal, sign, verify, or hash rung surfaced its own error — honored honestly.
    ComposedFailed,
};

/// What open() returns once provenance and confidentiality both hold: the recovered plaintext (a zero-copy slice into the caller's out buffer) and the
/// verified sender's two public keys, so a caller reads who sent it beside what they sent.
pub fn OpenedType(comptime p: pq.PqParams) type {
    return struct {
        plain: []const u8,
        sender_ed_public: [ed_public_len]u8,
        sender_mldsa_public: [pq.sign_public_len(p)]u8,
    };
}

/// seal_signed — seal `plain` TO the recipient's hybrid identity (the confidentiality onion) and SIGN the sealed frame with the sender's hybrid signature
/// (both families), writing a whole hybrid sealed-and-signed envelope into `out` and returning its length. Both `ephemeral_priv` (the inner X25519
/// ephemeral) and `m` (the outer KEM message) are FRESH randomness the caller supplies per message.
pub fn seal_signed(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    sender: *const hybrid.HybridType(p),
    recipient: *const hybrid.HybridType(p),
    ephemeral_priv: *const [ephemeral_bytes]u8,
    m: *const [seed_bytes]u8,
    plain: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSignedSealedError!u32 {
    // invariant (precondition): the plaintext fits the inner frame's bound, so the whole envelope fits and every length below stays a bounded u32.
    if (plain.len > max_plain_bytes) return error.PayloadTooLong;
    const hlen = header_len(p);
    // invariant (precondition): the caller's buffer holds at least the fixed header — the full check follows once the inner length is known.
    if (out.len < hlen) return error.OutTooSmall;

    // Write the inner hybrid sealed frame at out[hlen..], leaving room for the fixed header ahead of it.
    const inner_len = hsealed.seal_to_identity(p, recipient, ephemeral_priv, m, plain, out[hlen..]) catch |e| return switch (e) {
        error.OutTooSmall => error.OutTooSmall,
        else => error.ComposedFailed,
    };
    const inner = out[hlen .. hlen + inner_len];

    // Sign a BLAKE2b-512 digest of the inner sealed frame with the sender's HYBRID signature (Ed25519 + ML-DSA). Signing the digest (a fixed 64 bytes)
    // rather than the frame itself keeps the signed message within ML-DSA's bound at every plaintext length, and binds the signature to the exact
    // ciphertext by the hash's collision resistance.
    const digest = blake2b.hash512(inner) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const sig = hybrid.sign(p, sender, digest[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;

    // --- fixed header: magic ‖ sender Ed25519 public ‖ sender ML-DSA public ‖ Ed25519 signature ‖ ML-DSA signature ---------------------------------
    var off: u32 = 0;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < magic_len) : (i += 1) out[off + i] = magic[i];
    off += magic_len;

    var e: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly ed_public_len sender-Ed25519-public bytes.
    while (e < ed_public_len) : (e += 1) out[off + e] = sender.classical.public_key[e];
    off += ed_public_len;

    const pk_len = pq.sign_public_len(p);
    var k: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly pk_len sender-ML-DSA-public bytes.
    while (k < pk_len) : (k += 1) out[off + k] = sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key[k];
    off += pk_len;

    var s: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly ed_sig_len Ed25519-signature bytes.
    while (s < ed_sig_len) : (s += 1) out[off + s] = sig.ed[s];
    off += ed_sig_len;

    const ml_len = pq.sig_len(p);
    var d: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): copy exactly ml_len ML-DSA-signature bytes.
    while (d < ml_len) : (d += 1) out[off + d] = sig.mldsa[d];
    off += ml_len;

    // invariant (postcondition): the header was written to exactly the computed prefix length, and the whole envelope fits the buffer.
    assert(off == hlen);
    assert(hlen + inner_len <= out.len);
    return hlen + inner_len;
}

/// open_signed — recover the plaintext of a hybrid sealed-and-signed envelope, verify-before-trust: refuse unless the named sender matches the reader's
/// expectation on BOTH public keys and its HYBRID signature over the inner frame verifies (both families), THEN open the inner hybrid sealed frame with
/// the recipient's own hybrid identity. A wrong expected sender (UnexpectedSigner), a forged or one-family signature (BadSignature), a wrong recipient or
/// tampered ciphertext (OpenFailed) each refuse with no plaintext revealed. Returns the plaintext and the verified sender public keys.
pub fn open_signed(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    recipient: *const hybrid.HybridType(p),
    expected_sender_ed_public: *const [ed_public_len]u8,
    expected_sender_mldsa_public: *const [pq.sign_public_len(p)]u8,
    frame: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSignedSealedError!OpenedType(p) {
    const hlen = header_len(p);
    // invariant (precondition): the envelope is at least the fixed header, so every fixed field below has bytes to read.
    if (frame.len < hlen) return error.FrameTooShort;
    if (!bytes_eq(frame[0..magic_len], magic[0..])) return error.BadMagic;

    const pk_len = pq.sign_public_len(p);
    const ml_len = pq.sig_len(p);

    // Read the named sender's two public keys and refuse a sender the reader did not expect — before any signature check or agreement is opened.
    var off: u32 = magic_len;
    var sender_ed_public: [ed_public_len]u8 = undefined;
    var e: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly ed_public_len sender-Ed25519-public bytes.
    while (e < ed_public_len) : (e += 1) sender_ed_public[e] = frame[off + e];
    off += ed_public_len;

    var sender_mldsa_public: [pq.sign_public_len(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly pk_len sender-ML-DSA-public bytes.
    while (k < pk_len) : (k += 1) sender_mldsa_public[k] = frame[off + k];
    off += pk_len;

    if (!bytes_eq(sender_ed_public[0..], expected_sender_ed_public[0..])) return error.UnexpectedSigner;
    if (!bytes_eq(sender_mldsa_public[0..], expected_sender_mldsa_public[0..])) return error.UnexpectedSigner;

    // Read the two signatures into a HybridSig and the inner sealed frame (which runs to the end).
    var sig: hybrid.HybridSig(p) = undefined;
    var s: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly ed_sig_len Ed25519-signature bytes.
    while (s < ed_sig_len) : (s += 1) sig.ed[s] = frame[off + s];
    off += ed_sig_len;
    var d: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly ml_len ML-DSA-signature bytes.
    while (d < ml_len) : (d += 1) sig.mldsa[d] = frame[off + d];
    off += ml_len;
    // invariant (construction): the header cursor consumed exactly the fixed prefix.
    assert(off == hlen);
    const inner = frame[hlen..];

    // PROVENANCE FIRST — verify the sender's HYBRID signature over the BLAKE2b-512 digest of the inner frame; refuse a forgery (or a one-family break)
    // before touching the recipient's secret keys. The digest is recomputed here, so a tampered ciphertext yields a different digest and the signature
    // no longer verifies.
    const digest = blake2b.hash512(inner) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    const genuine = hybrid.verify(p, &sender_ed_public, &sender_mldsa_public, digest[0..], &sig);
    if (!genuine) return error.BadSignature;

    // CONFIDENTIALITY — open the inner hybrid sealed frame with the recipient's own hybrid identity; a wrong recipient or tampered ciphertext fails.
    const opened = hsealed.open_as_identity(p, recipient, inner, out) catch return error.OpenFailed;

    // invariant (postcondition): both provenance and confidentiality held — the plaintext is authentic, from the expected sender, sealed to this recipient.
    assert(genuine);
    var result: OpenedType(p) = undefined;
    result.plain = opened.plain;
    result.sender_ed_public = sender_ed_public;
    result.sender_mldsa_public = sender_mldsa_public;
    return result;
}

// ---- shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly a.len bytes, one per turn.
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic spread of test bytes — TEST plaintexts and TEST randomness only, never real entropy.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    if (buf.len > 0) buf[0] |= 1;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// BIP-39's own published Trezor vector 1 — a TEST phrase, never a real one.
const trezor_mnemonic = "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about";

/// check_param — prove the hybrid sealed-and-signed door for one parameter set: a confidential-and-authenticated round-trip across plaintext lengths, and
/// the refusals that prove both families guard both sides — a wrong recipient, a wrong expected sender, a tampered inner frame, and each half of the
/// hybrid signature tampered alone.
fn check_param(comptime p: pq.PqParams) !void {
    const test_coin: u32 = 7;
    const sender = try hybrid.from_mnemonic(p, trezor_mnemonic, "SENDER", test_coin, 0, 0);
    const recipient = try hybrid.from_mnemonic(p, trezor_mnemonic, "RECIPIENT", test_coin, 0, 0);
    const stranger = try hybrid.from_mnemonic(p, trezor_mnemonic, "STRANGER", test_coin, 0, 0);

    var esk: [ephemeral_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(esk[0..], 3);
    var m: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined; // TEST KEM message — fresh randomness in real use
    fill_test(m[0..], 19);

    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var out_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // 1. Round-trip across a spread of plaintext lengths: sealed to the recipient, signed by the sender, opened with both provenance and confidentiality,
    //    the recovered sender public keys equal to the sender's own.
    const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 16, 64, 500 };
    for (lengths) |plen| {
        var plain_buf: [500]u8 = undefined;
        const plain = plain_buf[0..plen];
        fill_test(plain, @truncate(plen +% 5));
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain, frame_buf[0..]);
        const opened = try open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain)) return error.PlainWrong;
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.sender_ed_public[0..], sender.classical.public_key[0..])) return error.SenderEdWrong;
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.sender_mldsa_public[0..], sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key[0..])) return error.SenderMlWrong;
    }

    // 2. Wrong recipient — the stranger cannot open a frame sealed to the recipient, even though the provenance is genuine: OpenFailed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 31);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open_signed(p, &stranger, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.WrongRecipientOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.OpenFailed) return error.WrongRecipientWrongError;
    }

    // 3. Wrong expected sender — a reader expecting the stranger refuses a frame the sender signed, before any agreement is opened: UnexpectedSigner.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 37);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &stranger.classical.public_key, &stranger.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.WrongSenderOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.UnexpectedSigner) return error.WrongSenderWrongError;
    }

    // 4. Tampered inner ciphertext — the hybrid signature no longer covers it: BadSignature, refused before any agreement is opened.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 41);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[header_len(p)] ^= 0x01; // a byte inside the inner sealed frame
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.TamperInnerOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.BadSignature) return error.TamperInnerWrongError;
    }

    // 5. A forger who breaks ONLY the classical family — a tampered Ed25519 signature byte — is caught by the hybrid verify: BadSignature.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 43);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        // The Ed25519 signature sits right after magic ‖ sender Ed25519 public ‖ sender ML-DSA public.
        const ed_sig_off = magic_len + ed_public_len + pq.sign_public_len(p);
        frame_buf[ed_sig_off] ^= 0x01;
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.TamperEdSigOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.BadSignature) return error.TamperEdSigWrongError;
    }

    // 6. A forger who breaks ONLY the post-quantum family — a tampered ML-DSA signature byte — is likewise caught: BadSignature.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 47);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const ml_sig_off = magic_len + ed_public_len + pq.sign_public_len(p) + ed_sig_len;
        frame_buf[ml_sig_off] ^= 0x01;
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.TamperMlSigOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.BadSignature) return error.TamperMlSigWrongError;
    }

    // 7. Bad magic and a too-short frame.
    {
        var plain_buf: [32]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 51);
        const flen = try seal_signed(p, &sender, &recipient, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[0] = 'X';
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.BadMagicOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.BadMagic) return error.BadMagicWrongError;
        frame_buf[0] = 'G'; // restore
        if (open_signed(p, &recipient, &sender.classical.public_key, &sender.post_quantum.sign.public_key, frame_buf[0 .. header_len(p) - 1], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.ShortOpened;
        } else |err| if (err != error.FrameTooShort) return error.ShortWrongError;
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    try check_param(pq.pq_small);
    try check_param(pq.pq_default);
    try check_param(pq.pq_large);

    print("GREEN crypto-hybrid-signed-sealed: the HYBRID sealed-AND-signed door authored in pure Rye — a message sealed TO a hybrid identity AND signed " ++
        "BY a hybrid identity, so one frame carries both confidentiality and provenance hardened on BOTH families at once: the classical Ed25519/X25519 " ++
        "world Comlink uses today, AND the post-quantum ML-DSA/ML-KEM world a lattice identity carries. It completes the hybrid surface — the hybrid " ++
        "identity signs on both families, the hybrid sealed door hides on both, and this door does both in one frame; the hybrid analog of " ++
        "crypto/kumara_pq_sealed. Authors NO new cryptography — confidentiality is crypto/hybrid_sealed's own onion (the classical X25519 box inside the " ++
        "post-quantum ML-KEM box), provenance is crypto/hybrid_identity's own hybrid signature (Ed25519 beside ML-DSA, genuine only when both verify); " ++
        "this module contributes only the signed envelope (magic GHS1 ‖ sender Ed25519 public ‖ sender ML-DSA public ‖ Ed25519 signature ‖ ML-DSA " ++
        "signature ‖ inner hybrid_sealed frame) opened verify-before-trust: the sender's two public keys are checked against the reader's expectation and " ++
        "the hybrid signature verified over a BLAKE2b-512 digest of the sealed frame, BEFORE either agreement is opened. Proven GREEN on the first metal " ++
        "pass at every tier (small ML-DSA-44/ML-KEM-512, default ML-DSA-65/ML-KEM-768, large ML-DSA-87/ML-KEM-1024): a confidential-and-authenticated " ++
        "round-trip across five plaintext lengths (empty → 500 bytes) recovering the plaintext and both verified sender keys, and six refusals — a wrong " ++
        "recipient fails OpenFailed, a wrong expected sender fails UnexpectedSigner before any agreement is opened, a tampered inner ciphertext fails " ++
        "BadSignature, a tampered Ed25519 signature byte fails BadSignature (a forger who breaks only the classical family is caught), a tampered ML-DSA " ++
        "signature byte fails BadSignature (a forger who breaks only the post-quantum family is caught), plus bad magic and truncation. The module's own " ++
        "functions call no std.crypto; each composed half is proven one rung down. Purely local over TEST hybrid identities from published vectors — no " ++
        "real key held, no network, no funds, no real device; both the X25519 ephemeral and the ML-KEM message must be fresh randomness in real use, " ++
        "constant-time timing-safety the named horizon, and sealing to or signing as the maintainer's own hybrid identity the custody gate. The hybrid " ++
        "surface now stands whole — a name that signs on both worlds, a message that hides on both, and one frame that proves who sent it and hides it " ++
        "from everyone else, falling only when both worlds fall at once.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
